body:
[Lemma 26 of arXiv:2507.06186, '2507.06186'] For every $c>0$,
there exists $\theta_c>0$ such that
for every $t\in(0,\theta_c)$,
\begin{align}

\sup_{x\in D,~\eps>0}\mbf E\left[\exp\Big(c\big(\be^\eps_t(Z)-\mbf E[\be^\eps_t(Z)]\big)\Big)\right]
<\infty
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}

\sup_{x\in D,~\eps>0}\mbf E\left[\exp\Big(c\be^\eps_{[0,t/2]\times[t/2,t]}(Z)\Big)\right]<\infty,
\end{align}
where $Z$ is either $B^{x,x}_t$ or $B^x$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

Let (a_n) be a bounded sequence of nonnegative reals. Then the Cesaro averages (1/N) sum_{n=1}^{N} a_n tend to 0 if and only if there is a subset J of the positive integers of DENSITY ZERO such that a_n tends to 0 as n tends to infinity along the complement of J. In words: convergence in density is convergence to 0 after deleting a negligible set of indices. The same statement holds with a_n replaced by |b_n| for a bounded complex sequence (b_n), and it is the standard bridge between mean ergodic statements and pointwise ones along a density-one set.
Boundedness of the sequence is used in the easy direction;
nonnegativity is a normalisation (apply to |b_n|).

[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] For any $\sigma \ge 0$, and $0<a<b\le1$, let us define the analytic function
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
Z(\cdot, \sigma : a,b): z \in D_{\sigma} (a,b) \mapsto Z(z,\sigma : a,b ):=\frac{1}{\sigma + f(0) + (1-z) f(0) (1/a -1/b)} \in \mathbb C 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
on the open disc $D_\sigma (a,b)$ of radius $R_\sigma (a,b)$, where
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
R_\sigma (a,b) = 1+\cfrac{\sigma + f(0)}{f(0) (1/a -1/b)} >1 \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Then for any $z\in D_{\sigma} (a,b)$ we have 
 \begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} Z^\varepsilon (z,\sigma : a,b) = Z(z,\sigma : a,b ) \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}

Let H be a Hilbert space (real or complex; the Banach-space version is identical with K* the Banach adjoint acting on the dual) and let K : H → H be a compact linear operator. Put T = I − K; the Hilbert adjoint is T* = I − K*, also identity minus compact. Then:
(a) ker T is finite-dimensional;
(b) range T is closed, range T = (ker T*)^⊥ and range T* = (ker T)^⊥;
(c) ker T = {0} if and only if range T = H (injective ⇔ surjective ⇔ bijective with bounded inverse);
(d) dim ker T = dim ker T* = codim range T < ∞, i.e. T is a Fredholm operator of index zero.

The alternative. EITHER the homogeneous equation u − Ku = 0 has only the trivial solution, and then u − Ku = f has exactly one solution for every f ∈ H, depending continuously on f; OR u − Ku = 0 has a d-dimensional space of solutions with 1 ≤ d < ∞, and then u − Ku = f is solvable if and only if ⟨f, w⟩ = 0 for every solution w of the adjoint homogeneous equation w − K*w = 0 (whose solution space is also d-dimensional); in that case the solution set is u_0 + ker T, an affine space of dimension d.

Spectral consequences. For each λ ≠ 0 apply the above to K/λ: σ(K) \ {0} consists of eigenvalues of finite multiplicity, is finite or a sequence tending to 0, and for such λ the generalised eigenspace ∪_m ker(λI − K)^m is finite-dimensional. If dim H = ∞ then 0 ∈ σ(K).

Where it fails. Compactness is essential: for the unilateral shift S on ℓ^2 (bounded, not compact) I − S is injective but not surjective (1 ∈ σ(S), the closed unit disc), so (c) fails. The alternative concerns I − K, not K itself: nothing is claimed at λ = 0; a compact K on an infinite-dimensional H is never surjective yet may be injective (diag(1/n) on ℓ^2), so 'K injective ⇒ K invertible' is false. In the second alternative the solvability condition is orthogonality to ker(I − K*), not to ker(I − K); they coincide when K is self-adjoint (or normal) but not in general. On an incomplete inner-product space closedness of the range and (c) can fail.
H a Hilbert space (complete); K : H → H compact linear
the statements concern λI − K with λ ≠ 0, normalised to λ = 1

Let $\mathbb{X}^1, \dots, \mathbb{X}^n$ be filtered processes. A coupling $\pi \in \mathcal{P}(\prod_{i=1}^n \Omega^i)$ is called multicausal if its marginal on $(\Omega^i, \mathcal{F}^i)$ is $\mathbb{P}^i$ for each $i$, and for every $i \in \{1, \dots, n\}$ and $t \in \{1, \dots, T-1\}$, the $\sigma$-fields $\mathcal{F}_0^1 \otimes \dots \otimes \mathcal{F}_T^i \otimes \dots \otimes \mathcal{F}_0^n$ and $\mathcal{F}_t^1 \otimes \dots \otimes \mathcal{F}_t^n$ are conditionally independent given $\mathcal{F}_0^1 \otimes \dots \otimes \mathcal{F}_t^i \otimes \dots \otimes \mathcal{F}_0^n$ under $\pi$. The set of multicausal couplings is denoted by $\operatorname{Cpl}_{\mathrm{mc}}(\mathbb{X}^1, \dots, \mathbb{X}^n)$.
