[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Assume that $m, n, k\in\mathbb{N}$ satisfies $k\le \min\{m, n\}$. Let $0<p\le 1$ and $\varepsilon>0$. Then for every matrix $X\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ of rank $k$ and any constant $r$ such that $$k\ge r\ge C_1\log \frac{mn}{\eta}\cdot\max\{ \frac{ p}{\varepsilon^2}, \frac{1 }{\varepsilon} \},$$ then Algorithm~\ref{alg:low_rank} outputs a matrix $Y\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ of rank no more than $r$ such that with probability at least $1-\eta$,
    \begin{align}
        \Vert X-Y\Vert_{\infty}\le \frac{k\varepsilon}{p\sqrt{mn}}\sqrt{\mu_\mathrm{col}\mu_\mathrm{row}}\cdot\Vert X\Vert_{l_2\to l_2},
    \end{align}
    where $\mu_\mathrm{col}$ and $\mu_\mathrm{row}$ are the coherence of the column and row spaces of $X$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

Let X = R^2 minus the origin, identified with the nonzero complex numbers C*, and fix a base point x_0. For a CONTINUOUS loop gamma : [0,1] -> X with gamma(0) = gamma(1), write gamma(t) = |gamma(t)| e^{2 pi i theta(t)} with theta : [0,1] -> R continuous (such a lift exists and is unique once theta(0) is chosen, by the path lifting property of the covering map t -> e^{2 pi i t} from R onto the unit circle). The WINDING NUMBER is n(gamma) = theta(1) - theta(0), an integer independent of the choice of lift.

Then gamma -> n(gamma) induces a group ISOMORPHISM from pi_1(X, x_0) onto the integers Z. Consequently:
 (i) two loops at x_0 are homotopic rel endpoints in X if and only if they have the same winding number;
 (ii) since Z is abelian, conjugacy classes are singletons, so two loops (not necessarily based at the same point) are FREELY homotopic in X if and only if they have the same winding number; free homotopy classes of loops correspond bijectively to Z;
 (iii) a loop is null-homotopic in X exactly when its winding number is 0;
 (iv) the winding number is additive under concatenation and changes sign under reversal;
 (v) for a piecewise C^1 loop, n(gamma) = (1 / (2 pi i)) * (contour integral of dz/z along gamma), and for a loop avoiding an arbitrary point p the same statements hold for R^2 minus {p} with dz/(z - p).

SHARP EDGES. (1) Only CONTINUITY is needed; smoothness or rectifiability is required only for the integral formula in (v). (2) The homotopy must stay inside X at all times: a homotopy that is allowed to cross the puncture trivializes everything, which is precisely why the invariant exists. (3) The result is special to ONE puncture in dimension two. For R^n minus a point with n >= 3 the space is simply connected, every loop is null-homotopic and there is no winding number at all. (4) For TWO or more punctures the classification FAILS in this form: pi_1 of the plane minus two points is the free group on two generators, which is non-abelian, and the pair of winding numbers is only the image in the abelianization H_1 = Z x Z. The commutator loop a b a^{-1} b^{-1} has winding number 0 about both punctures yet is not null-homotopic. (5) Winding number is a homotopy invariant of the loop in X, not of its image set: two loops with the same image can have different winding numbers (traverse the circle once or twice).
X = R^2 minus one point, identified with C*; loops are continuous maps of [0,1] with equal endpoints
homotopies are required to take values in X throughout (they may not cross the puncture)
the winding number is defined via a continuous lift through the covering map t -> e^{2 pi i t}; no differentiability is assumed
the contour-integral formula additionally assumes the loop is piecewise C^1 (or rectifiable)
the classification is asserted for exactly one puncture in the plane, not for several punctures or for higher dimensions

[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Let $\xi$ be a random variable such that for $r\ge r_{0}$
	\begin{align}
		\Vert \xi\Vert_{L_r}\le \sum_{k=1}^{m}C_{k}r^{\beta_{k}}+C_{m+1},\nonumber
	\end{align}
	where $C_{1},\cdots, C_{m+1}> 0$ and $\beta_{1},\cdots, \beta_{m}>0$. Then for $t>0$,
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb{P}\big\{ \vert \xi\vert>e(mt+C_{m+1}) \big\}\le e^{r_{0}}\exp\Big(-\min\big\{\big(\frac{t}{C_{1}}\big)^{1/\beta_{1}},\cdots, \big(\frac{t}{C_{m}}\big)^{1/\beta_{m}}\big\}\Big).\nonumber
	\end{align}

