In the potential outcomes framework (Neyman-Rubin causal model), for each unit $i$ in a population and binary treatment $T_i \in \{0, 1\}$, there exist two potential outcomes: $Y_i(1)$, the outcome if the unit receives treatment ($T_i = 1$), and $Y_i(0)$, the outcome if the unit receives control ($T_i = 0$). The observed outcome is $Y_i = T_i Y_i(1) + (1 - T_i) Y_i(0)$.
Stable Unit Treatment Value Assumption (SUTVA)
Treatment is binary T \in {0, 1}

[Prop 3 (Optimality of Theorem~\ref{Theo_main2} when $p_{1}=\cdots=p_{2}=1$) of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Let $\eta_{1}, \cdots, \eta_{n}\stackrel{i.i.d.}{\sim}\mathcal{W}_{s}(\alpha)$ and $A$ be a symmetric diagonal-free matrix. Then for $0<\alpha\le 1$ and $t\ge 0$
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb{P}\{\vert \eta^\top A\eta-\mathbb{E}\eta^\top A\eta\vert\ge t    \}\ge C(\alpha)\exp(-c(\alpha)f_{2}(t)),\nonumber
	\end{align}
	where $\eta=(\eta_{1}, \cdots,\eta_{n})^{\top}$, $C(\alpha), c(\alpha)$ are  positive constants depending only on $\alpha$, and $f_{2}(t)$ is defined in \eqref{Eq_intro_f2}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

In a tensor-triangulated category $\mathcal{T}$, the homological Bousfield class of an object $E$, denoted $\langle E \rangle$, is the class of all objects $X \in \mathcal{T}$ such that $E \otimes X \neq 0$. Two objects $E, F$ are Bousfield equivalent (written $\langle E \rangle = \langle F \rangle$) if they have the same class of acyclics: $E \otimes X = 0 \iff F \otimes X = 0$ for all $X \in \mathcal{T}$.
\mathcal{T} is a tensor-triangulated category
E \in \mathcal{T}

