(ii) If u in L^2 solves the equation, comparing Fourier coefficients gives (e^{2 pi i q alpha} - 1) u-hat(q) = f-hat(q). Since alpha is irrational, e^{2 pi i q alpha} is not 1 for q nonzero, so u-hat(q) is determined for q nonzero and u-hat(0) is free: this is uniqueness up to a constant, and it also forces the mean-zero condition on f (take q = 0). For existence, define u-hat(q) by that formula. Because f is C^infinity, for every N there is c_N with |f-hat(q)| <= c_N |q|^{-N}; by (i), |u-hat(q)| <= (4C)^{-1} c_N |q|^{tau - N}. Taking N > tau + k + 2 makes sum |q|^k |u-hat(q)| finite for every k, so the Fourier series and all its termwise derivatives converge absolutely and uniformly, and u is C^infinity and solves the equation. The Sobolev estimate is the same computation with weights.

