A Bousfield localization functor $L: \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{T}$ on a tensor-triangulated category is called smashing if the canonical map $X \otimes L(\mathbb{I}) \to L(X)$ is an isomorphism for every object $X \in \mathcal{T}$. Equivalently, $L$ preserves arbitrary coproducts.
L is a Bousfield localization functor
\mathbb{I} is the unit object of \mathcal{T}

On $U_T=U\times(0,T]$ let $Lu=-a^{ij}(x,t)u_{x_ix_j}+b^i(x,t)u_{x_i}+c(x,t)u$ with real coefficients, $a^{ij}=a^{ji}$. The operator $\partial_t+L$ is uniformly parabolic if there is $\theta>0$ with $a^{ij}(x,t)\xi_i\xi_j\ge\theta|\xi|^2$ for all $(x,t)\in U_T$, $\xi\in\mathbb R^n$ (the heat operator is $a^{ij}=\delta^{ij}$, $b=0$, $c=0$). $C^2_1(U_T)$ denotes functions $u$ with $u,Du,D^2u,u_t$ continuous in $U_T$. A function $u\in C^2_1(U_T)$ is a subsolution if $u_t+Lu\le0$ in $U_T$ and a supersolution if $u_t+Lu\ge0$. Sign conventions matter: with this $L$, maximum principles need $c\equiv0$ or $c\ge0$ (see the pitfall on the zeroth-order term).
a^{ij} = a^{ji}
a^{ij} xi_i xi_j >= theta |xi|^2 with theta > 0 on U_T

[Definition 4.5 of arXiv:2507.06186, '2507.06186'] For any $\ka,\eps,t>0$, we let $\mc K_{\ka,\eps}(t)$ denote the integral operator on $L^2(D)$ with kernel
\begin{align}

\mc K_{\ka,\eps}(t;x,y)=p_t(x-y)\mbf E_B\left[\mbf 1_{\{\tau_D(B^{x,y}_t)>t\}}\exp\left(-\ka\int_0^t\xi_\eps\big(B^{x,y}_t(r)\big)\d r\right)\right],\qquad x,y\in D,
\end{align}
where we assume that $B^{x,y}_t$ and $\xi_\eps$ are independent, and we use $\mbf E_B$ to
denote the (conditional) expectation with respect to the randomness in $B$ only.

Let $\mathcal Hu=\partial_tu-\mathrm{div}(A\nabla u)$ be uniformly parabolic with constants $\lambda,\Lambda$ and measurable coefficients, and let $u$ be a weak solution (or a nonnegative weak subsolution) of $\mathcal Hu=0$ in $Q_{2r}=Q_{2r}(x_0,t_0)=B_{2r}(x_0)\times(t_0-4r^2,t_0]$. Then $\sup_{t_0-r^2<t\le t_0}\int_{B_r(x_0)}u^2(x,t)\,dx+\iint_{Q_r}|\nabla u|^2\,dx\,dt\le\frac{C(n,\lambda,\Lambda)}{r^2}\iint_{Q_{2r}}u^2\,dx\,dt.$ The same inequality holds with $u$ replaced by $(u-k)_+$ for any constant $k$ when $u$ is a subsolution; that truncated form is the engine of De Giorgi's iteration.
A measurable, uniformly parabolic with constants lambda, Lambda
u a local weak solution (or nonnegative weak subsolution) in the backward cylinder Q_{2r}
u in L^infty_t L^2_x cap L^2_t H^1_x locally on Q_{2r}

Let T be a consistent theory in a countable language \mathcal{L}, and for each n, let \Sigma_n(x) be a non-principal type. Then T has a countable model \mathcal{M} that omits every \Sigma_n.
Countable language \mathcal{L}
Non-principal types \Sigma_n

Let $(S, d)$ be a metric space with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra, and let $\mu_n$ ($n \ge 1$) and $\mu$ be Borel PROBABILITY measures on $S$. The following are equivalent:
  (a) $\mu_n \Rightarrow \mu$, i.e. $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded continuous $f: S \to \mathbb{R}$;
  (b) $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded uniformly continuous $f$ (it suffices to take bounded Lipschitz $f$);
  (c) $\limsup_n \mu_n(F) \le \mu(F)$ for every closed set $F \subset S$;
  (d) $\liminf_n \mu_n(G) \ge \mu(G)$ for every open set $G \subset S$;
  (e) $\mu_n(A) \to \mu(A)$ for every Borel set $A$ with $\mu(\partial A) = 0$ (a $\mu$-continuity set), where $\partial A = \overline{A} \setminus \mathrm{int}(A)$;
  (f) $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded Borel function $f$ whose set of discontinuity points $D_f$ satisfies $\mu(D_f) = 0$.
The same equivalences hold on $S = \mathbb{R}$ with (e) restricted to half-lines: $\mu_n \Rightarrow \mu$ iff the distribution functions converge, $F_n(x) \to F(x)$, at every continuity point $x$ of $F$.

Sharp edges. (1) The equal total mass (probability measures, or at least finite measures with $\mu_n(S) \to \mu(S)$) is essential: for finite measures with $\mu_n(S) \not\to \mu(S)$, (c) alone or (d) alone does not give (a); e.g. $\mu_n = 0$ satisfies (c) against any $\mu$. Passing between (c) and (d) uses complements together with $\mu_n(S) = \mu(S) = 1$. (2) In (e) the boundary condition is on the LIMIT measure $\mu$, not on the $\mu_n$: $\delta_{1/n} \Rightarrow \delta_0$ but $\delta_{1/n}((0, \infty)) = 1 \not\to 0 = \delta_0((0,\infty))$ because $\delta_0(\partial (0,\infty)) = \delta_0(\{0\}) = 1$. (3) Bounded test functions only: $\mu_n = (1-1/n)\delta_0 + (1/n)\delta_n \Rightarrow \delta_0$ on $\mathbb{R}$ yet $\int x \, d\mu_n = 1 \not\to 0$; nothing is asserted about moments without uniform integrability. (4) The inequalities in (c) and (d) can be strict: $\delta_{1/n} \Rightarrow \delta_0$ with $F = \{0\}$ gives $\limsup \mu_n(F) = 0 < 1 = \mu(F)$.
(S, d) is a metric space with its Borel sigma-algebra
mu_n and mu are Borel probability measures (equal total mass 1 is used in passing between closed-set and open-set forms)
test functions in (a),(b) are bounded; boundary-null condition in (e) is with respect to the limit measure mu

Let f be an orientation-preserving homeomorphism of the circle and let F be any lift of f to the real line. The limit rho(F) = lim_n (F^n(x) - x)/n exists, is independent of x, and its class modulo 1 - the rotation number rho(f) - is independent of the lift; it is invariant under topological conjugacy by an orientation-preserving homeomorphism. Moreover rho(f) is RATIONAL if and only if f has a periodic orbit, and when rho(f) = p/q in lowest terms every periodic orbit has period exactly q. When rho(f) is irrational, f has no periodic point, every orbit has the same combinatorial order as an orbit of R_{rho}, and f is semi-conjugate to the rigid rotation R_{rho}; the semi-conjugacy is a conjugacy exactly when f has no wandering interval.
Orientation-preserving and merely a HOMEOMORPHISM - no smoothness is used here;
smoothness enters only in Denjoy's theorem to upgrade the semi-conjugacy to a conjugacy.

[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Assume that $\{\delta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ and $\{\zeta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ are two independent sequences of random variables, where $\delta_{i}\sim\textnormal{Bernoulli}(p)$ are independent and $\zeta_{i}$ are independent $\alpha$-sub-exponential random variables with mean $0$ and variance $1$.
    Consider a random vector $\xi=(\xi_{1}, \cdots, \xi_{n})$  with $\xi_{i}=\delta_{i}\cdot\zeta_{i}$. Let $A$ be an $m\times n$ fixed matrix. Then for $0<\alpha\le 2$ and $t\ge 0$,
    \begin{align}
        \mathbb{P}\Big\{ \big\vert \Vert A\xi\Vert_{2}-\sqrt{p}\Vert A\Vert_{F} \big\vert>L^{2}(\alpha)t \Big\}\le 2\exp\Big\{-c(\alpha)\min \big\{\big(\frac{t}{\Vert A\Vert_{l_2\to l_2}}\big)^{2}, \big(\frac{t}{\Vert A\Vert_{l_2\to l_2}} \big)^{\alpha} \big\}    \Big\},
    \end{align}
    where $L(\alpha)=\max_{i}\Vert \zeta_i\Vert_{\Psi_{\alpha}}$ and $c(\alpha)>0$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

