Let (X, Sigma) be a measurable space, let lambda be a finite nonnegative measure on Sigma, and let (mu_n) be a sequence of countably additive finite signed (or complex) measures on Sigma with each mu_n absolutely continuous with respect to lambda. Suppose the limit mu(E) = lim_n mu_n(E) exists in the scalars for every E in Sigma. Then the family (mu_n) is UNIFORMLY absolutely continuous with respect to lambda: for every eps > 0 there is delta > 0 such that lambda(E) < delta implies |mu_n(E)| < eps for every n. Consequently (Nikodym's theorem) the set function mu is itself countably additive and absolutely continuous with respect to lambda.
Countable additivity of every mu_n;
a single finite dominating lambda;
convergence of mu_n(E) for EVERY measurable E, not merely on a generating algebra.

[Corollary 5 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] For any $t>0$ and any $0<a\le b\le1$, the probability distribution $P^\varepsilon_c( \cdot \, : \, t,a,b)$ over $\mathbb N_0$ converges in law to a Poisson distribution with parameter 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
f(0) \, t\,  \lambda_* ( [a,b]) = f(0)\,  t \, \int_a^b \cfrac{dx}{x^2} = f(0) \, t \,  (a^{-1}- b^{-1}) \  . 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, we have that, for any $n\ge 0$, the function
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\Phi_n^\varepsilon: r \in[0,\infty) \mapsto \mathbb P ( e_1^\varepsilon \ge r, N^\varepsilon_{(0,r]} ([a,b]) = n) \in [0,1]
\end{split}
\end{equation}
converges uniformly on any compact interval $[0,t]$, $t>0$, as $\varepsilon$ vanishes to 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\Phi_n: r \in [0,\infty) \mapsto e^{-r f(0)} e^{-r \lambda_* ([a,b]} \frac{(r \lambda_* ([a,b]))^n}{n!} \in [0,1] \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}

