body:
Let U be an open set in R^d and let {K_n} be a sequence of precompact open sets exhausting U with closure(K_n) subset K_{n+1}. Let X_t^n be the unique global strong solution of an SDE with coefficients agreeing with mu, sigma on K_n. Define tau_n = inf{t >= 0 : X_t^n not in K_n}. Then for m > n, X_t^m = X_t^n on [0, tau_n] almost surely, and X_t := X_t^n for t <= tau_n defines a unique local strong solution on [0, tau_infinity) with tau_infinity = lim_{n -> infinity} tau_n.
Pathwise uniqueness holds for truncated Lipschitz systems
Compact exhaustion {K_n}

Let f: {-1, 1}^n -> {0, 1} be a Boolean function with Var(f) = p * (1 - p). Then there exists an index i in {1, ..., n} such that Inf_i(f) >= c * Var(f) * (log n / n) for an absolute constant c > 0.
f: {-1, 1}^n -> {0, 1}
Var(f) > 0

When the forcing satisfies B_1 / B_0 << lambda_{ell_0} for an index ell_0 << N, the condensation bound forces 2 tilde{E}[U_0 - V_0] / B_0 << 1, demonstrating that the stationary distribution concentrates almost all of its enstrophy and energy on the two lowest Fourier modes x_1 and x_2 in the inviscid limit.
B_1/B_0 << lambda_{ell_0}
ell_0 << N

A random variable X has Cauchy distribution \text{Cauchy}(x_0, \gamma) with location parameter x_0 \in \mathbb{R} and scale parameter \gamma > 0 if and only if its characteristic function is \phi_X(t) = \exp\left(i x_0 t - \gamma |t|\right) for all t \in \mathbb{R}.
x_0 \in \mathbb{R}
\gamma > 0

[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] Let $e^\varepsilon_2$ be the stopping time defined by 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
e^\varepsilon_2 = \inf \{ t \ge e_1^\varepsilon \; ; \; X_t^\varepsilon =0 \} \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}
We have that
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} \mathbb P (e^\varepsilon_2 > t)  = e^{f(1) t}  \ ,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
i.e. $(e^\varepsilon_2)_{\varepsilon>0}$ converges in law to an exponential random variable with parameter $-f(1)$.

[Lemma 3.6 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] For any $\delta>0$, there exists $K<\infty$ such that 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\limsup_{\varepsilon \to 0} \mathbb E \left[ \mathbb Q_0^\varepsilon ([0,t] \times [\delta,1]) \right] \le K  \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}

